{
  "name": "Vacuum Fluctuation Harvester",
  "icon": "theoreticalsciences:vacuum_harvester",
  "category": "theoreticalsciences:spacetime",
  "sortnum": 65,
  "pages": [
    {
      "type": "patchouli:spotlight",
      "item": "theoreticalsciences:vacuum_harvester",
      "title": "Vacuum Fluctuation Harvester",
      "text": "Holds a bubble of false vacuum open in the Quantum Foam and catches the virtual particles that flicker through it. What it catches depends on where it stands and how it is tuned.$(br2)$(thing)Tier 5$() machine."
    },
    {
      "type": "patchouli:text",
      "text": "Quantum field theory says empty space is never quite empty: $(thing)virtual particles$() borrow energy for an instant and vanish again. In the Foam they flicker densely enough to catch.$(br2)This controller is too complex for a crafting grid. Build it on the $(item)Femtoscale Assembly Line$() - check JEI for the exact parts."
    },
    {
      "type": "patchouli:text",
      "text": "Field notes. It only works in the $(thing)Quantum Foam$(). Each virtual particle has a $(thing)resonance$() (0-100) that changes from place to place. The harvester catches whichever particle its dial is tuned closest to, and the closer the better."
    },
    {
      "type": "patchouli:text",
      "text": "So I take a $(item)Resonance Probe$() to where the harvester stands and read the four values. Then I either move the harvester or retune it, to choose what it makes. Two wasted afternoons before I accepted that the readings are local."
    },
